# Chavruta.AI — TORAH QUESTIONS eval v1: real questions, phrased the way a person actually asks.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#   The other sets ask in terms already close to the corpus. This one deliberately does not. Every
#   question here is worded like a person speaking — "what's the argument between Rashi and Tosafot
#   about…", "may I… on Shabbat" — while the corpus is written in the language of the sugya. That
#   gap is the system's dominant failure mode, measured 2026-08-12 on one chunk (Rashi on Sukkah
#   41a, the מקדש העתיד comment):
#
#       "מקדש העתיד בנוי ומשוכלל…"        → rank 2
#       "אין בנין בית המקדש בלילה…"       → rank 12
#       "מה המחלוקת בין רש"י לתוספות…"    → not in the top 50
#
#   Same chunk, same index, same embedding. So this set measures the bridge, not the corpus.
#
# HOW THE expected_refs WERE CHOSEN
#   Not guessed. Each was found by probing the live collection in the SOURCE's own wording and
#   recording what came back — so every ref here is known to exist and known to be reachable. A
#   failure is therefore always a query-understanding failure, never a missing text.
#
# FORMAT  {"qid","question","lang","expected_refs":[…],"intent","note","history":[…]}
#   expected_refs are segment-prefix matched, so "Rashi_on_Sukkah.81" matches ".81.11.2".
#   expected_refs == []  ⇒  honest no-source must hold (the work is genuinely not in the corpus).
#
# RUN  python scripts/run_eval.py --dataset eval/torah_questions_v1.jsonl --retrieval-only
#
{"qid": "tq-01", "question": "מה המחלוקת בין רש\"י לתוספות לגבי בניית בית המקדש ואיך אפשר ליישב אותה", "lang": "he", "expected_refs": ["Rashi_on_Sukkah.81"], "intent": "explain", "note": "the reported failure. Rashi ד\"ה אי נמי: המקדש העתיד יורד בנוי משמים."}
